Michael Turniansky wrote:
> My favorite activities lojban-wise is translating what random people
> in the hallway or on the radio say... Like, I fantasize about trekking
> out to lojbab's house (he lives about an hour or two drive away),

You are welcome to do so (but give us warning).  Where do you live?

> meeting him, and saying "coi lojbab .i ioro'a do finti la lojban .i lo
> nu penmi do cu pluka .i yyyy  mi mo'u tavla do . i co'o", getting back
> in my car and driving back for 1-2 hous.  Cause I have a feeling
> that's pretty much how it would go. 

If you are interested, I'm sure we could tackle something meatier in the 
translation department.

I don't know if we are up to it this year, but there is nothing wrong 
with holding Lojban gatherings in multiple locations (whether we call 
them all "LogFest" or something else).  Obviously, we cannot expect 
everyone to attend all of them, though perhaps we could choose a common 
weekend and communicate electronically between them.

(Nora and I had been thinking about trying for Michigan this year if a 
LogFest were held there. SF is a bit harder in requiring a plane flight 
and a good deal more money, and I'm not sure how well I would travel by 
plane these days, but most of my family is there.)

If something is held in SF, I would suggest to Robin that he (or 
someone) use our address list to generate mailing labels, and have 
someone send postcards to people on the list in CA.  There were a 
hundred odd people in SF back when I was maintaining the mailing list, 
and another several dozen in LA.  Not everyone actively monitors the 
online lists, and the last time I did an event in SF (a class), I think 
we had close to 20 people with minimal promotion.

